# terminfo.src
# Master source for terminfo database
# Terminfo definitions are public domain and not covered by copyright.
# Many of the entries in this file are derived from the ncurses project.

# This master source contains the most commonly used terminal types.
# Ladybridge Systems can supply definitions for many other terminals
# for users with devices that are not defined here. For further assistance
# e-mail support@ladybridge.com

# Installation of a new release of SD will overwrite this file and recompile
# all terminal definitions included in it.  It is therefore recommended that
# any new or modified definitions are stored in a separate file so that they
# can easily be recompiled after an upgrade. The install process will look for
# a file named terminfo.mods in the SDSYS account directory and, if found, will
# compile it after the contents of this file.

# If a terminal definition is modified, it must be compiled using the sdtic
# utility.

# Each entry consists of a series of comma separated tokens.  There may be
# spaces at the start and end of a line or after a comma. Where a line ends
# with a comma, the entry continues on the next line. Blank lines and lines
# commencing with a # character are ignored.

# The first token in an entry consists of a series of terminal type names
# separated by | characters.  The last component of this sequence is a
# desciption of the entry, not a terminal type name.

# (The AccuTerm terminal types, those with a -at suffix, were removed on 13 Oct 2026: SD no longer has any code
# that depends on AccuTerm's private escape sequences.)

# (The width and depth in an entry are what a session with no terminal to ask takes.  They were 120 and 36
# and are 140 and 42 since 18 Oct 2026, SD's default terminal size.  The 132-column entries keep their
# depth of 36 and ms-vt100 its 25.  A real terminal's own size overrides all of them.)

# The remaining tokens define the capabilities of the terminal using the
# standard terminfo names for these. There are three types of entry:
#    Booleans     name
#    Numbers      name#123
#    Strings      name=xxxxxxx

# The following special character sequences are recognised in strings:
#    \b    Backspace       (char 8)
#    \e    Escape          (char 27)
#    \f    Formfeed        (char 12)
#    \l    Linefeed        (char 10)
#    \n    Linefeed        (char 10)
#    \r    Carriage return (char 13)
#    \s    Space           (char 32)
#    \t    Tab             (char 9)
#    \^    Caret           (^)
#    \\    Backslash       (\)
#    ^x    Ctrl-x          (chars 0 - 31)
#    \nnn  Octal char value

# The terminfo standard provides ten strings (u0 to u9) for user use. SD uses
# these as defined below:
#    u0 - u7  Available for user defininition [ @(-100) to @(-107) ]
#    u8       Asynchronous command [ @(-108) ]
#    u9       Synchronous command [ @(-109) ]

# SD sends the string defined by the is1 capability on initial entry and
# whenever the terminal type is changed.


ansi|ansi/pc-term compatible with color,
    am, mir, msgr, mc5i,
    cols#140, it#8, lines#42, colors#8, pairs#64, ncv#3,
